r them  he'd go to jail for perjury and she'd be  impeached they think about all the  options and they realize there's nothing  they can do so larry offers to call a  press conference to confess he tells her  that she can pretend that she didn't  know make it like he betrayed her too  and that would solve their problem this  is all his fault really and there's some  great setup here because it's nice to  see that he would take responsibility  and even though there's no way around it  that she still doesn't want him to have  to do it because she realizes what kind  of sacrifice this is for him to make of  course at the moment that he gets up to  the podium and gets ready to make the  announcement she steps in to take his  place she tells the american people and  the world that she lied to them and she  comes out and admits she's gay she  delivers a moving speech that it's not  even necessary to summarize because at  this point in the story we understand  where this character is the sacrifices  that she's made what she's risking by  doing this now and why she's telling the  world that she's gay and that she's been  that way since the day she was born  since we've been following this  character for two and a half decades the  scene really delivers jodie balfour  really sells it in her performance and i  really like the moment at the end when  she shares the look with larry because  he's kind of been an annoying character  for most of this season because of this  problem that he caused and it's just  another reminder of everything they've  been through together it was nice to see  margot watching and the speaker of the  house's reaction and then of course  seeing pam if that wasn't enough to hit  you in the heart we watch as everyone at  happy valley learns about her executive  order to allow gay men and women to  openly serve in the military and that  she'll bestow the presidential medal of  freedom on will the actor does an  amazing job of delivering his reaction  to 